**Subject:** SEMINAR SERIES: Wednesday 10th June 2026 

**SEMINAR SERIES**

**Wednesday 10th June 2026**

**Venue:** Auditorium A, Shirley Hall, Chancellors’ Building

**Time:** 1200-1300

Speaker: Craig Lygate, University of Glasgow

Title: **“** **A tale of two metabolites: the therapeutic potential of creatine and homoarginine in heart disease”**

Creatine and homoarginine are intrinsically linked metabolites. Creatine matches energy supply and demand in the heart and elevating it could improve recovery from ischaemia. Homoarginine has no known biological requirement, yet low levels are associated with poor outcomes in cardiovascular disease, and augmentation may be beneficial via pleiotropic mechanisms that remain elusive.

Host: Cecile Benezech

Social: Pizza – 1300-1330 – The Larder

**_Short Bio:_**Craig did his PhD in cardiovascular physiology at the University of Glasgow under the supervision of John Gordon and Anna Dominiczak. He moved to Oxford University in 2000 for his first post-doc in the lab of Stefan Neubauer, where he worked on cardiac energetics, and established murine models of ischaemia and heart failure. Craig was promoted to Associate Professor in 2014 running a BHF-funded research group within the Department of Cardiovascular Medicine. In 2024, he returned to Glasgow as a Reader in Myocardial Biology at the School of Cardiovascular & Metabolic Health. He was recently awarded a British Heart Foundation programme grant to continue his studies on creatine and homoarginine metabolism.

